A host resolves an MCP server's headers without holding its process, once
for a burst of conversations.

Every conversation a host starts or wakes opens its own MCP connections,
and each resolves the server's headers afresh, running any command they
name with execSync. A command that fetches a token through a few
programs can take about 0.27 s, during which every conversation in the
host stands still; ten waking together stand still ten times.

Run the command through exec instead, and let every connection opened
within 30 s share that one run, so a burst runs it once. Its value is
held in memory for those 30 s, so a command whose token lives shorter
than that would hand out a stale one. A run that resolved nothing isn't
kept, so the next connection tries again, and
clearing the config value cache clears these runs too. A transport
factory may now answer with a promise, and a connection shut down while
its headers resolve stops there. Only MCP transports take this path:
provider keys and headers resolve as before.

Apply only to the host's private pi build; ordinary interactive pi stays
unchanged.

